#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tests/ci-plan — the CI planner's own proof (px37, px40, tl29). No QEMU: python3 and bash.

    tests/ci-plan

  P1  the tiers by event (tl29): a trunk push with nothing green at its
      head, the nightly and a dispatch are all (every suite, every leg); the `full-matrix` label is full,
      the merge gate (every suite on the merge legs, native BIOS), and all
      beside a FULL path or with no paths to read; another label's
      `labeled` event is a noop; a dispatch can ask for full or pr, and
      one naming suites is partial, on its tier input's legs. The
      `full-matrix` label's own event is a noop where the head's paths
      already choose every suite or a FULL path: the push's run at that
      head is the full (or all) run, and one run a head is enough.
      `macos-run` is in no full run: a pull request's merge gate waits
      for no macOS runner; a push (all), the nightly, a FULL-path
      pull request (all) and a pr run that chooses the proof carry it.
  P2  the pr tier by path, one change per area: the network's suites for
      kernel/tcp, sound's for user/play, nothing for notes; a kmain, a
      test, a user program and a shared device select their readers.
      CI's own files select every suite, on the pr leg, which is the
      merge gate's shape: that run is named full.
  P3  FULL wins: the toolchain pins, the harness, the kernel's entry,
      link script and Limine configuration, the kernel-wide modules (the
      scheduler, the timer, the interrupt and syscall entries), the
      kernel's core (px40's modules and their assembly, and every other
      kernel or boot path every suite runs: no such rule of the map is
      ALL, so the gate is one all run and the push a noop), and any path no rule knows
      make a pull request's run all (every leg), alone or beside a core
      change; a pull request whose changed paths cannot be read is all too.
  P4  the wiring of .github/workflows/ci.yml: every suite job needs the
      plan and runs only when chosen; every test that takes a leg gets
      $PAX_LEGS and accepts --tier and --firmware; the `tier` job needs
      every job and runs unless cancelled; cancel-in-progress is for
      pull requests only and every other run has a concurrency group
      of its own run id; the trunk push, the nightly and the label are
      triggers; the dispatch's tier input is all, full or pr, all by
      default; a job of its own, after the tier job and on a red
      schedule only, runs tools/nightly-red, and only that job may write
      issues.
  P14 no job a pull_request (or pull_request_target) event can reach may
      write, by its own permissions or the workflow's: a job whose `if:`
      can be true on such an event, for any value of what the `if:` cannot
      decide (failure(), cancelled(), needs.*, a label, an input), reads
      only. The check is shown to say no to a job that runs unless
      cancelled, and to pass one that runs on a red schedule alone.
  P15 every checkout whose job then runs a command keeps no git
      credentials (`persist-credentials: false`): no later step needs
      them, since what CI fetches goes through tools/fetch-retry into
      repositories of its own. A job that ever needs them for a later git
      command is named here with why (none today); checkouts of other
      repositories are counted apart.
  P16 every step that runs apt-get ends when apt stalls (pax#540): it has a
      `timeout-minutes` of its own, at most APT_STEP_MINUTES, and its update
      is `sudo tools/apt-update` (stopped after two minutes of silence and
      tried once more), before any `apt-get install`; no bare `apt-get
      update` is left. The check is shown to say no to a step with no limit,
      one with a limit over the bound, one with a bare update and one that
      installs before it updates, and to pass the shape it asks for.
  P8  what a trunk head already has (tools/ci-covered, against a
      stand-in `gh`): a green `tier all` check run at the head is all, a
      green `tier full` alone is full, a red one or another app's is
      none, an API failure is none (so the push runs every leg), and no
      REPO or SHA is refused. A push plans from it: all green, noop;
      full green or none, all (every leg: the release legs alone are no
      leg set twelve suites can run, pax's trunk push runs 45003384558
      and 45018671496). The
      nightly too: all green, noop, but the weekly schedule runs all.
  P6  the verdict (`tools/ci-plan --verdict`, the `tier` job): green for
      an all run whose `legs` output is absent (GitHub drops an empty
      output; run 38344726280 was red on exactly that), a full run on the
      merge legs, a pr run, a partial run and a noop; red for a chosen
      suite failed, skipped or cancelled, a suite that ran unasked, a
      failed plan, a plan with no suites, and (tl29) a tier whose shape
      does not hold: a full run that chose macos-run or an all run of
      fewer suites than every one, a full run of fewer than every other,
      a full run on every leg (px37's full, now all), an all run on the
      merge legs, a pr run on every leg, a partial run of no suite, a
      tier the planner never names.
  P7  the red nightly's issue (tools/nightly-red, against a stand-in
      `gh`, tl29): no open issue opens one, labelled nightly-red, under
      the one title; an open one gets a comment and no new issue; a label
      that already exists is no failure; unset variables are refused.
  P9  a kernel change whose run includes the firmware suite (a narrow pr
      run, or an every-suite one named full) runs it on the release legs
      too (the planner's also_release); nothing more for other changes,
      kernel-wide ones (all has those legs), pushes, the nightly or a
      dispatch.
  P10 the KVM split: sixteen suites, named, open the hosted runner's
      /dev/kvm (tools/kvm-open, right after checkout, an unnamed step)
      when the plan says kvm=yes, and no other job does; the plan says
      yes for every event but the weekly never-skip run and a dispatch
      asking accel=tcg, which run every suite on TCG.
  P11 the native leg: tools/ci-plan's NATIVE_LEG names every suite
      whose job boots the native leg beside the plan's legs (a step
      outside its $PAX_LEGS step naming `--tier native`, native BIOS or
      native UEFI, in a job that builds no native image of its own), and
      no other from ci.yml; an entry from a suite's script names words
      that script holds; and the planner refuses a plan whose legs leave
      out the native tier (or the firmware an entry names) for a suite
      it chose, while every leg, the merge legs and the pr leg pass.
  P12 the job's token for tools/fetch-retry (PAX_FETCH_TOKEN): the
      workflow gives every job its own, read-only; a job whose token can
      write (the `nightly-red` job's issues) empties it; nothing else sets it or
      FETCH_RETRY_OWN; and a workflow that runs on pull_request_target
      gives it no run. The check says no to each of those, on ci.yml
      changed to break it.
  P5  every tracked path is known to the map or read by a suite. An
      unknown path would make every pull request touching it run every
      leg; now the pull request that adds one is red here instead.
  P13 BusyBox from the cache, under busybox-<the .deb's sha256 in
      user/busybox.pin>, in three shapes. A job whose suite cannot run
      without it (tests/usb-stick's mpx3-console, mpx5-jobs) restores it,
      fetches it in a step of its own before the suite (a failed fetch
      stops the job there, by name) and saves it when the restore missed.
      mpx5-pelt alone adds no named step: tests/ci-budgets.py cannot time
      a new named step of it whose plan job it reddens, so the pin read,
      the restore and the save are unnamed steps around the suite, whose
      own fetch stops it by fetch-busybox's line. The suites that fetch it
      for a live Linux side alone (tests/mpx6-ext2, mpx6-nvme) restore it
      and fetch nothing that could stop them. Every suite that runs
      tools/fetch-busybox is on one of those lists.

Exit 0 when every check passes, 1 otherwise.
"""
